INT. GRAIN ELEVATOR - BASE LEVEL - NIGHT

Dust hangs in the work light. Conveyor belts, silent. A hopper the size of a church.

CASIMIR "CASS" DUDA (40s, night foreman, canvas coat) walks the catwalk with a clipboard. Counting bins. Boots ring on steel.

Something shifts in the grain below.

CASS
Fenwick?

No answer. He taps his flashlight against his palm.

FENWICK OKORO (20s, new hire, hard hat too big) climbs up from a ladder at the far end. Wipes his hands on his overalls. Looks pale.

FENWICK
Bin six is warm.

CASS
Grain heats. Settles.

FENWICK
It's breathing, Cass.

Cass hears it now. A slow rattle, like a sleeper in the next room. Cass swings his beam over the side. Fenwick leans out beside him, and the grain below rises in a smooth dome, as if something is turning over under a blanket, and Cass grabs Fenwick by the collar while the light jumps across the rafters and a third person steps out from behind a support column, silent, in a yellow slicker.

The newcomer is MARGUERITE LAU (60s, inspector's badge, white braid). She raises a hand for quiet.

MARGUERITE
Both of you. Back to the stairs. Walk.

CASS
Who are you?

MARGUERITE
The reason this elevator was condemned in 1987.

The dome in the grain sinks. Slowly the rattle stops.

FENWICK
Is it gone?

MARGUERITE
It is listening.

They walk to the stairs together, step for step, not looking back. Behind them a single kernel rolls down the slope of the hopper and clicks against the steel.
